import{c as o,$ as n,Q as i,j as r,g as t,o as s,n as l,m as c}from"./chunks/framework.Ce_49mA8.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Personal Operations","description":"Practical guide to managing the ledger of major personal income, capital expenses, business dividends, and the net worth balance delta in OmniCraft ERP.","frontmatter":{"title":"Personal Operations","description":"Practical guide to managing the ledger of major personal income, capital expenses, business dividends, and the net worth balance delta in OmniCraft ERP.","datePublished":"2026-10-28","dateModified":"2026-11-16","order":18},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/moy-kapital/operations.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/moy-kapital/operations.md","lastUpdated":1794787200000}'),d={name:"docs/spaces/moy-kapital/operations.md"},p={id:"personal-operations-ledger-of-major-financial-movements",tabindex:"-1"};function u(g,e,h,m,f,y){const a=n("DocIcon");return i(),r("div",null,[t("h1",p,[s(a,{name:"capital"}),e[0]||(e[0]=l(" Personal Operations: Ledger of Major Financial Movements ",-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#personal-operations-ledger-of-major-financial-movements","aria-label":'Permalink to "Personal Operations: Ledger of Major Financial Movements"'},"​",-1))]),e[2]||(e[2]=c('<p>The <strong>Personal Operations</strong> tab serves as the primary recorder for key financial milestones within the maker&#39;s personal wealth space. Unlike the day-to-day cash flow ledger (Cash Flow / DDS) inside a workshop, this tab is strictly dedicated to fundamental capital movements: withdrawing business dividends, recording external earnings, selling personal vehicles, purchasing real estate, funding higher education, or executing substantial family investments.</p><p>Maintaining this ledger systematically frees artisans from the daily burden of household budgeting. The system automatically computes ordinary day-to-day living expenses using the balance delta method, requiring makers to log only those transactions that substantially alter the overarching structure of personal net worth.</p><hr><h2 id="accounting-philosophy-why-you-never-log-a-cup-of-coffee" tabindex="-1">Accounting Philosophy: Why You Never Log a Cup of Coffee <a class="header-anchor" href="#accounting-philosophy-why-you-never-log-a-cup-of-coffee" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accounting Philosophy: Why You Never Log a Cup of Coffee&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A guiding design principle of OmniCraft ERP is conserving the craftsman&#39;s time and mental bandwidth for creative production. Conventional personal finance software typically obligates users to record every receipt, supermarket purchase, and transport fare manually. In practice, this micromanagement invariably triggers burnout, unrecorded gaps, and total abandonment of financial oversight.</p><p>OmniCraft ERP implements an alternative engineering methodology — <strong>calculating discretionary living expenses via the balance delta</strong>:</p><ol class="craft-list-steps"><li><div><strong>Baseline Capital on Accounts:</strong><br> Total opening balance across bank cards, deposits, vaults, and cash reserves. </div></li><li><div><strong>+ Total Earning Pool:</strong><br> Consolidated workshop profits + external personal earnings + investment net returns. </div></li><li><div><strong>− Major Recorded Personal Expenses:</strong><br> Targeted expenditures logged in the ledger (medical procedures, taxes, education, vehicles). </div></li><li><div><strong>− Actual Current Liquid Balance on Accounts:</strong><br> Real closing balance across all tracked cards and accounts at period end. </div></li><li><div><strong>= Automatically Computed Everyday Living Expenses:</strong><br> Discretionary household living burn calculated in reverse without manual receipt sorting. </div></li></ol><p>Makers simply update their actual liquid balances periodically in the <strong>&quot;Accounts &amp; Wallets&quot;</strong> tab and log significant financial milestones in this ledger. The algorithm contrasts generated net profits against real account appreciation, automatically attributing the remaining delta to household living expenditures without manual receipt tracking.</p><hr><h2 id="balance-delta-mathematics-and-untracked-expense-calculation" tabindex="-1">Balance Delta Mathematics and Untracked Expense Calculation <a class="header-anchor" href="#balance-delta-mathematics-and-untracked-expense-calculation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Balance Delta Mathematics and Untracked Expense Calculation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The &quot;My Capital&quot; module utilizes an exact mathematical model to evaluate discretionary household burn. It aggregates financial performance across all manufacturing and service workspaces alongside personal bank accounts and investment assets.</p><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Balance Delta</span><div class="formula-heading">Automated Calculation of Craftsman Household Expenses</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-formula-display"><span class="math-var">Living Expenses</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-var">Total Earning Pool</span><span class="math-op">−</span><span class="math-group">(</span><span class="math-var">Major Recorded Expenses</span><span class="math-op">+</span><span class="math-var">Net Worth Delta</span><span class="math-group">)</span></div></div><div class="formula-card-bottom"><p>The total earning pool combines net profit from workspaces, logged personal earnings, and net investment yield. If net worth appreciated by less than total earnings over a period, the difference is recognized as household living expenses without requiring manual receipt collection.</p></div></div><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Accounting Mechanics: Protecting Delta Integrity</p><p>If a maker sells an old van for $15,000 and deposits the proceeds into a personal bank account, liquid assets spike instantly. If this transaction is not logged in the operations ledger as an extraordinary personal income, the system would either conclude that the manufacturing workshop produced an uncharacteristic surge in sales or distort living cost calculations. Logging the event communicates the exact capital source to the calculation engine.</p></div><hr><h2 id="workspace-layout-two-pane-architectural-design" tabindex="-1">Workspace Layout: Two-Pane Architectural Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#workspace-layout-two-pane-architectural-design" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workspace Layout: Two-Pane Architectural Design&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Personal Operations interface separates historical audit trails from transactional data entry:</p><div class="craft-window-frame" data-title="Two-Pane Architecture: Operations Registry and Transaction Form"><table class="craft-matrix-table" style="margin:0;"><thead><tr><th style="width:55%;">Operations Registry (Audit Trail)</th><th style="width:45%;">Record Transaction (Input Form)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><ul class="craft-feature-list" style="margin:0;"><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Timestamp:</strong> Precise date and time of record entry.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Classification:</strong> Personal finance category mapping.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Amount:</strong> Visual indicator (+ emerald, − crimson).</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Context Note:</strong> Memo description and source of funds.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Action:</strong> Instant record deletion with metric recalc.</div></li></ul></td><td><ul class="craft-feature-list" style="margin:0;"><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Operation Date:</strong> Interactive calendar date picker.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Operation Type:</strong> Outside income or capital expense.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Category Selector:</strong> Context-aware taxonomy dropdown.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Amount &amp; Note:</strong> Numeric input and contextual memo.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"></span><div><strong>Action Button:</strong> Commit transaction to local database.</div></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id="left-panel-operations-table" tabindex="-1">Left Panel: Operations Table <a class="header-anchor" href="#left-panel-operations-table" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Left Panel: Operations Table&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The left workspace contains the chronological audit log of all recorded personal transactions:</p><ul><li><strong>Date Stamp:</strong> records the calendar date and timestamp of the transaction;</li><li><strong>Category:</strong> contextual category label matching the transaction type;</li><li><strong>Amount Column:</strong> indicates the monetary value in your base currency with an emerald <code>+</code> prefix for inflows and a crimson <code>-</code> prefix for outflows;</li><li><strong>Comment Memo:</strong> contextual narrative regarding counterparty, source, or intended objective;</li><li><strong>Deletion Control:</strong> immediate removal of erroneous entries with instant dashboard recalculation;</li><li><strong>Documentation Shortcut <code>[?]</code>:</strong> directs your browser to this online guide.</li></ul><h3 id="right-panel-record-operation-card" tabindex="-1">Right Panel: Record Operation Card <a class="header-anchor" href="#right-panel-record-operation-card" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Right Panel: Record Operation Card&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A compact entry form allows you to register capital events within seconds without encountering unnecessary input clutter.</p><hr><h2 id="form-field-specifications" tabindex="-1">Form Field Specifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#form-field-specifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Form Field Specifications&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When logging a financial event, complete the following fields:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Field Name</th><th style="text-align:left;">Interface Control</th><th style="text-align:left;">Purpose and Validation Format</th><th style="text-align:left;">Example Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>&quot;Operation Date&quot;</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Adaptive Date Picker</td><td style="text-align:left;">Effective calendar date of debit or credit</td><td style="text-align:left;"><em>Selected calendar date</em></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>&quot;Operation Type&quot;</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Dropdown Selector</td><td style="text-align:left;">Cash flow direction: personal income or capital expense</td><td style="text-align:left;"><em>Personal Income (Salary, freelance)</em></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>&quot;Category&quot;</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Dynamic Dropdown</td><td style="text-align:left;">Contextual classification article from structured catalog</td><td style="text-align:left;"><em>Passive Income (deposits, rent)</em></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>&quot;Operation Amount&quot;</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Numeric Text Field</td><td style="text-align:left;">Monetary transaction sum filtered for numerical values</td><td style="text-align:left;"><em>2,500.00</em></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>&quot;Comment&quot;</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Multiline Text Field</td><td style="text-align:left;">Contextual narrative explaining the capital movement</td><td style="text-align:left;"><em>Q3 workshop net profit dividend draw</em></td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="category-classification-reference" tabindex="-1">Category Classification Reference <a class="header-anchor" href="#category-classification-reference" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Category Classification Reference&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The category dropdown automatically rebuilds its options depending on the active operation type:</p><h3 id="_1-inflow-categories-personal-income" tabindex="-1">1. Inflow Categories (Personal Income) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-inflow-categories-personal-income" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Inflow Categories (Personal Income)&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>&quot;Main Salary&quot;:</strong> dependable external payroll if the artisan balances craft operations with employment at an external firm;</li><li><strong>&quot;Freelance / Gigs&quot;:</strong> consulting honorariums, technical assessments, specialized guest workshops, or private tutoring;</li><li><strong>&quot;Passive Income (deposits, rent)&quot;:</strong> recurring interest payments from high-yield accounts, real estate rental receipts, or public market stock dividends;</li><li><strong>&quot;Blogging / Donations / Ads&quot;:</strong> digital platform monetization, sponsor partnerships, and community support donations;</li><li><strong>&quot;Other Personal Income (gifts)&quot;:</strong> family inheritances, cash gifts, old debt settlements, or capital realized from one-off sales of private goods.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-outflow-categories-major-expense" tabindex="-1">2. Outflow Categories (Major Expense) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-outflow-categories-major-expense" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Outflow Categories (Major Expense)&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>&quot;Mortgage / Large Loans&quot;:</strong> scheduled amortization installments or principal prepayments for workshop properties, land plots, or family homes;</li><li><strong>&quot;Major Purchases (tech, furniture)&quot;:</strong> household appliances, domestic furnishings, residential renovations, heating/cooling installations, or private automobiles;</li><li><strong>&quot;Health / Education&quot;:</strong> medical treatments, dental care, masterclasses, specialized technical textbooks, or university tuition;</li><li><strong>&quot;Travel and Vacations&quot;:</strong> family holidays, seasonal retreats, and physical recuperation following demanding delivery cycles;</li><li><strong>&quot;Other Major Expenses&quot;:</strong> tax assessments, legal retainers, insurance premiums, or targeted philanthropic gifts.</li></ul><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Craftsman&#39;s Advice: Logging Business Dividends</p><p>When your workshop generates surplus net earnings, transferring profit from the workshop cash register into your personal account involves two straightforward steps:</p><ol><li>In the workshop workspace <strong>&quot;Cash Flow (DDS)&quot;</strong> tab, record an expense categorized as <em>“Profit Distribution / Dividends”</em>;</li><li>In the <strong>&quot;Personal Operations&quot;</strong> tab, register an income entry under <em>“Passive Income”</em> or <em>“Other Personal Income”</em> with the note <em>“Workshop dividend distribution”</em>. This procedure ensures flawless operational clarity: the workshop accurately notes the cash outflow, while personal net worth registers true owner wealth appreciation.</li></ol></div><hr><h2 id="practical-workshop-scenarios" tabindex="-1">Practical Workshop Scenarios <a class="header-anchor" href="#practical-workshop-scenarios" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Practical Workshop Scenarios&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="scenario-1-sale-of-a-workshop-delivery-van" tabindex="-1">Scenario 1: Sale of a Workshop Delivery Van <a class="header-anchor" href="#scenario-1-sale-of-a-workshop-delivery-van" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scenario 1: Sale of a Workshop Delivery Van&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The craftsman upgrades logistical equipment: an aging delivery van is sold for $12,000, and the buyer remits payment directly to the owner&#39;s bank card.</p><ol><li>Open the operations entry card;</li><li>Select type <strong>&quot;Personal Income (Salary, freelance)&quot;</strong>;</li><li>Choose category <strong>&quot;Other Personal Income (gifts)&quot;</strong>;</li><li>Enter sum <strong>$12,000.00</strong>;</li><li>Provide comment: <em>“Proceeds from sale of delivery van prior to machinery upgrade”</em>;</li><li>Click <strong>&quot;Submit Operation&quot;</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Outcome:</strong> The system registers $12,000 as external capital. On the capital summary dashboard, this sudden liquidity jump is not mistakenly treated as workshop manufacturing revenue.</p><h3 id="scenario-2-personal-capital-injection-into-workshop-owner-loan" tabindex="-1">Scenario 2: Personal Capital Injection into Workshop (Owner Loan) <a class="header-anchor" href="#scenario-2-personal-capital-injection-into-workshop-owner-loan" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scenario 2: Personal Capital Injection into Workshop (Owner Loan)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>To acquire a precision CNC cutter, the craftsman transfers $5,000 from personal savings into the workshop operational cash register.</p><ol><li>In the personal operations ledger, record a major expense under <strong>&quot;Other Major Expenses&quot;</strong> with comment: <em>“Working capital injection for CNC purchase”</em>;</li><li>In the workshop <strong>&quot;Cash Flow (DDS)&quot;</strong> tab, record an income entry categorized as <em>“Owner Capital Injection”</em>;</li><li>The workshop treasury is funded for procurement, while consolidated net worth eliminates the danger of counting the same capital twice.</li></ol><hr><h2 id="registry-management-and-pagination" tabindex="-1">Registry Management and Pagination <a class="header-anchor" href="#registry-management-and-pagination" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Registry Management and Pagination&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To maintain seamless UI responsiveness regardless of transaction history depth, the operations table incorporates responsive pagination:</p><ul><li><strong>Page Limit:</strong> transactions render in fixed row batches;</li><li><strong>Navigation Controls:</strong> <strong>&quot;Previous&quot;</strong> and <strong>&quot;Next&quot;</strong> arrow buttons enable effortless browsing;</li><li><strong>Page Counter:</strong> clearly indicates present standing (<em>“Page 1 of 5”</em>).</li></ul><h3 id="empty-state-and-demonstration-mode" tabindex="-1">Empty State and Demonstration Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#empty-state-and-demonstration-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Empty State and Demonstration Mode&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When configuring a new installation without historical records, the table transitions into a helpful onboarding view:</p><ul><li>Presents a receipt icon and descriptive guidance;</li><li>The <strong>&quot;Demo Mode&quot;</strong> action opens an interactive dialog illustrating standard ledger best practices with simulated data.</li></ul><h3 id="removing-erroneous-entries" tabindex="-1">Removing Erroneous Entries <a class="header-anchor" href="#removing-erroneous-entries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Removing Erroneous Entries&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If an entry was submitted with an incorrect sum or classification:</p><ol><li>Locate the transaction row within the audit table;</li><li>Click the trash icon in the actions column;</li><li>The transaction is purged from the database, and capital indicators recompute within fractions of a second.</li></ol><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Safeguarding Operational Integrity</p><p>Never use the personal operations ledger to record workshop raw material purchases like leather, lumber, metal hardware, or adhesives. Operational production disbursements must proceed strictly through warehouse receiving manifests and the workspace cash flow ledger. Violating this rule compromises unit cost calculations and skews workshop gross margins.</p></div>',54))])}const w=o(d,[["render",u]]);export{b as __pageData,w as default};
